Bookworm (Debian 12)


----

sudo apt install ffmpeg luarocks
sudo luarocks install bit32
sudo luarocks install lua-periphery

gpu_mem=128 in /boot/firmware/config.txt

-----



    Get and run kernel patch installation scripts

wget https://github.com/rniwase/sdtv2csi/raw/master/rpi/install_patch.sh
sudo bash install_patch.sh

    Set dtoverlay in config.txt

# for Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm:
sudo bash -c 'echo "dtoverlay=adv728x-m,adv7280m=1" >> /boot/firmware/config.txt'

    Reboot the system and check for devices.

sudo reboot
sudo v4l2-ctl --all -d /dev/video0

Operation check by FFmpeg

    Connect NTSC composite video signal to VIDEO_IN pin header AIN1-GND

    Install FFmpeg

sudo apt update
sudo apt install ffmpeg

    Video recording

# Record full video including blanking line
ffmpeg -an -video_size 720x507 -r 29.97 -i /dev/video0 -c:v rawvideo -vf realtime -t 5 out.asf
# Record video with blanking lines cropped
ffmpeg -an -video_size 720x507 -r 29.97 -i /dev/video0 -c:v rawvideo -vf realtime,crop=720:480:0:27 -t 5 out_crop.asf





------


This PCB is based on the sdtv2csi design (https://github.com/rniwase/sdtv2csi) and is made specifically for the Raspberry Pi Zero, but also works great with the Pi 3 and 4. It features the ADV7280 video decoder, which converts analog video signals (CVBS, S-Video) into digital formats. Works great for video capture or streaming.

There are two user-programmable buttons for extra control and customization.

Keep in mind that this is the second iteration of the project and it not the fully finished product. Sadly, the included Neopixel doesn't working because of a pinout error. This will be fixed in the next version.

The board comes fully assembled but for the 2 header connects (PI and Video). The reason being that you might prefer different style for your specific need.

You will receive the board, a PI 40pin socket header, a 16pin socket header and a short ribbon connector.

https://github.com/lerabot/dvx-nebula
